      "id": 1,
      "text": "Broadwell was platted in 1856 by William Broadwell and Jacob Eisiminger when the Chicago–Springfield railroad arrived.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broadwell%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village name came from William Broadwell's landowner name, not from Jacob who declined the honor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broadwell%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "An inn called Tantivy stood three‑quarters of a mile east of the future site around 1840, later known as Tantivy Lodge until the 1950s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broadwell%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Broadwell sits on the former southbound lanes of historic U.S. Route 66, now a preserved stretch of the old highway.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
